K1=C/'K45-01_transcription.tex';K2=C/'K45-02_transcription.tex';C1=C/'C45-01_transcription.tex'
cl(89,[],45,[4,5],K1,'10–63','Lemma1: H² vanishing for specified commutative groups implies H¹ surjectivity for group epimorphisms.','The argument reduces lifting a torsor along G→G′ to neutralising a twisted-kernel obstruction, using normalizers and commutative devissage. Its written smoothness and kernel-linearity restrictions matter.','proof sketched','C','Grothendieck–Springer over perfect cd≤1 fields is established; Flicker–Scheiderer–Sujatha Introduction and §3 specify the published hypotheses. They do not automatically validate this intermediate lemma over arbitrary imperfect fields.',['AG45-FSS1998'],confidence='medium')
cl(90,[],45,[5],K1,'65–67','Finite commutative H² vanishing suffices for the hypotheses.','Passage from finite commutative coefficients to tori and abelian varieties requires torsion and multiplication-isogeny exact sequences; all relevant twists and extensions must satisfy the vanishing.','proof sketched','C','Perfect-field versions follow from standard Galois cohomology and multiplication isogenies; Harari §2.4 warns why ordinary cd_p of an imperfect field does not control flat μ_p-cohomology.',['AG45-FSS1998','AG45-Harari2024'],confidence='medium')
cl(91,[],45,[5],K1,'65–67','Positive cohomology of smooth commutative algebraic groups is torsion.','For a smooth commutative finite-type group over a field, positive-degree Galois cohomology classes are killed by finite restriction/corestriction after a finite separable splitting extension.','stated without proof','E','Classical Galois-cohomology restriction/corestriction supplies this torsion assertion; Harari §§2.2–2.3 gives the mechanism.',['AG45-Harari2024'],'A continuous cocycle is defined over a finite extension where it becomes a coboundary; cor∘res equals the extension degree. The smoothness/Galois interpretation is essential.')
cl(92,[],45,[6,7],K1,'70–101','Purely inseparable extension and Frobenius kernel diagram.','Relative Frobenius factors the relevant purely inseparable pullback; the cohomology kernel/image statement requires the exact sequence0→kerF→G→G^(p)→0 in the flat topology and commutative coefficients in higher degrees.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 VIIA §4/§7 treats Frobenius and height-one kernels; the manuscript’s variance and higher pointed-set notation are retained rather than simplified.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'],confidence='medium')
cl(93,[],45,[7,8],K1,'98–145','Lemma3: reduce epimorphisms to smooth and radicial kernels.','Factorisations by smooth/reduced parts and purely inseparable quotients can reduce a torsor-lifting problem, provided the resulting subgroups descend and the twist is formed before applying the induction.','proof sketched','C','SGA3 VIA/VIIB gives the smooth and infinitesimal group theory; nonperfect reduced-subgroup descent is not automatic and the line126 variable change needs checking.',['AG45-SGA3VIB','AG45-SGA3VIIB'],confidence='medium')
cl(94,[],45,[8,9],K1,'136–154','Lemma4: radicial-kernel lifting from commutative vanishing.','The proposed reduction of a noncommutative height-one kernel to a commutative kernel uses a Cartan normalizer step which is not valid as written. The lemma is not thereby refuted, but its proof is incomplete.','proof sketched','C','SGA3 VIIA §7 identifies height-one groups with restricted Lie algebras; the searched published perfect-field H² theorem does not supply this exact imperfect-field reduction.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-FSS1998'],confidence='medium')
cl(95,[],45,[9],K1,'147–153','N_G(h)K=G by conjugacy of Cartan subalgebras.','This asserted equality fails for G=SL₂ over an algebraically closed field of characteristic p>2, K=ker(F_G), and h the diagonal Cartan in LieK=sl₂.','false as stated','B','Classical conjugacy under the smooth ambient group is not conjugacy under its finite infinitesimal kernel. SGA3 VIIA §7 makes the infinitesimal distinction precise.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-Conrad2014'],'N_G(h) has dimension1, K has dimension0, so N_G(h)K has dimension1, while G has dimension3. The field is algebraically closed, hence every finite commutative H² vanishing assumption in the lemma is satisfied.')
cl(96,[],45,[10],K1,'169','H^i(k,α_p)=0 «tt i».','In flat cohomology, H¹(k,α_p)=k/k^p, which can be nonzero; H^i(k,α_p)=0 for i≥2 over a field. Even H⁰ should not be included indiscriminately as a functor.','false as stated','A','The Frobenius exact sequence0→α_p→G_a→G_a→0 and affine quasi-coherent acyclicity give the correction. SGA3 VIIA handles α_p, and Stacks’ flat/étale comparison supplies the cohomological framework.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-StacksQC'],'For k=F_p(t), t is not a pth power by its valuation1, so its class in H¹ is nonzero. H^i(k,G_a)=0 for i>0 gives higher vanishing.')
cl(97,[],45,[10],K1,'157–169','Lemma5: Br(K′)=0 for every finite separable K′/k gives vanishing H^i(k,H), i≥2, for finite commutative H.','The stated Brauer hypothesis is stronger than bare Galois cd_p≤1 in characteristicp. Devissage must include finite étale factors and nontrivial forms, not only multiplicative type and α_p.','proof sketched','C','Harari Theorem2.53 and Remark2.52 settle the Galois part and expose the imperfect-field distinction; the complete finite-flat lemma was not matched with these exact quantifiers in an opened source. It is not claimed false from the α_p line alone.',['AG45-Harari2024','AG45-FSS1998'],confidence='medium')
cl(98,[],45,[10],K1,'171–172','cd(k)≤1 implies H¹(G)→H¹(G′) surjective for every epimorphism.','For perfect k of cd≤1 and smooth algebraic groups in the appropriate exact sequence, the obstruction is neutral. A formulation allowing arbitrary finite-flat kernels over imperfect k needs a stronger flat cohomological-dimension convention.','stated without proof','B','Grothendieck’s theorem, published by Springer1966 Thm3.5 and reproved by Flicker–Scheiderer–Sujatha1998, supplies the perfect-field version. Bare imperfect-field notation remains unresolved.',['AG45-FSS1998','AG45-Harari2024'],date='Springer1966 Thm3.5; FSS received2Sep1997, publishedJuly1998. Manuscript undated; first circulation not determined.')
cl(99,[],45,[11,14,15],K1,'174–178;215–265','Every algebraic kernel has singleton neutral H².','Over a perfect field k with cd(k)≤1, every continuous k-kernel in a smooth algebraic group has exactly one H² class and it is neutral.','proof sketched','B','Springer1966 Thm3.5 is explicitly attributed to Grothendieck; FSS1998 §§1,3 repairs continuity conventions and gives a new proof. The manuscript’s larger site/finite-flat formulation is not silently included.',['AG45-FSS1998'],date='Published1966; independent proof received1997, published1998.')
cl(100,[],45,[12,13],K1,'180–214','Can a kernel K on a site be represented by a sheaf of groups? Normalizer and quotient reductions.','A band need not have a chosen representing group; a neutralisation problem is encoded by gerbes and reduction data. The page’s I(K), c(K) and subgroup-class sheaf need precise definitions before a universal obstruction degree can be assigned.','open on the pages','C','Giraud’s nonabelian cohomology formalises bands and gerbes; CCMT2013 §3.5 rechecks the relevant exact sequences. Neither source makes the uncertain I/Z reading harmless.',['AG45-CCMT2013','AG45-Giraud1971'],confidence='medium')
cl(101,[],45,[14],K1,'221–239','[K,K]=K, «en particulier sans centre»; radicial line [K,K]=1.','Perfectness alone does not imply trivial centre. A nonabelian simple composition factor would be centreless. The radicial line as transcribed says commutative and centreless, which forces triviality.','false as stated','E','The finite-group correction is elementary and compatible with an intended induction on simple factors; the actual ink may contain a missing qualification or wrong1.',['AG45-FSS1998'],'math_checks.py:perfect_not_centerless exhausts SL₂(F₅): order120, derived subgroup order120, centre{±I}. No reliance on an unverified group-table lookup.')
cl(102,[],45,[17],K1,'267–300','G is defined over k but Z=centre=radical is not when char2 and a∉k².','The scheme-theoretic centre Z given by x²−ay²=u² is defined over k. Over kbar its reduced identity component is x−√a y=u and may fail to descend as a smooth subgroup.','false as stated','B','Modern pseudo-reductive theory distinguishes scheme centre from geometric smooth radical; Conrad’s descent discussion confirms the need for this distinction. The reconstructed geometric-radical statement is not the literal displayed scheme assertion.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'The defining ideal has k-coefficients. Over kbar in characteristic2 it becomes(x−√a y−u)², so its reduced ideal needs√a. Taking a nonsquare in k=F₂(t) verifies the descent obstruction.')
cl(103,[],45,[18],K2,'2–37','Normalizer Lie-algebra equalities and flag immersion, then rank-one warning.','The differential of the map Bor(G)→Grass(LieG), B↦LieB, has kernel LieN_G(LieB)/LieB. Equality of these Lie normalizers is the required infinitesimal criterion and can fail in bad characteristic.','corrected by himself later','A','SGA3 XXII §5 and Conrad §5.2 establish the flag scheme; they do not imply that this particular Grassmannian map is always immersive.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXII'],'For SL₂ in characteristic2 the Borel Lie algebra is span(H,X); every bracket with Y remains in that span, so its Lie normalizer is all sl₂, larger than b.')
cl(104,[],45,[20,21,26],K2,'54–100;226–259','Grassmannian flag map; closed immersion iff self-normalizing Lie algebra.','For a proper flag variety, an injective map on geometric points with vanishing relative tangent kernels yields an unramified radicial proper morphism, hence a closed immersion. The geometric injectivity and tangent test must both be checked.','proof sketched','A','EGAIV’s proper-monomorphism criterion and SGA3’s flag scheme give the corrected criterion; bad-characteristic failure of the tangent condition is real.',['AG45-EGAIV4','AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(105,[],45,[19],K2,'38–53','Closed subgroup as stabilizer of a line; preceding cancelled assertion.','A closed subgroup of an affine algebraic group can be realised as the stabilizer of a line in a finite-dimensional representation (Chevalley’s theorem). The cancelled finiteness statement for multiplicative subgroups needs its missing hypotheses.','stated without proof','E','Chevalley’s stabilizer theorem is recalled in Conrad’s algebraic-group notes; it is different from saying every multiplicative-type subgroup is finite.',['AG45-ConradGroups'])
cl(106,[],45,[21],K2,'88–100','Cancelled uniqueness of a subgroup from its Lie algebra containing a Cartan.','In positive characteristic the Lie algebra generally does not determine a smooth connected subgroup; root weights and separability matter. The page withdraws the broad uniqueness step.','corrected by himself later','C','SGA3 root-subgroup theory supplies a classification with full root datum and group structure, not Lie algebra alone.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXII'])
cl(107,[],45,[22],K2,'101–122','A smooth subgroup whose fibres are Borels is a Borel subgroup.','For a reductive group scheme, a smooth closed subgroup with geometric fibres Borel is a Borel subgroup; the Borel locus in a parabolic family is open and closed.','stated without proof','A','Conrad Theorem5.2.11 and SGA3 XXII5.8.3 prove the precise relative statement.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXII'])
cl(108,[],45,[23,24],K2,'123–225','Cancelled root-weight criteria for rationality of the radical; exclusions2,3,5.','The proposed root-weight/Cartan criterion for descending a geometric radical is incomplete and partially cancelled. No theorem with its exact undefined group F and characteristic exclusions can be recovered.','corrected by himself later','F','SGA3 and modern pseudo-reductive structure separate radical descent from root decomposition. Search did not identify the precise abandoned formulation.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],confidence='low')
cl(109,[],45,[25],K2,'215–225','Equal reductive and unipotent ranks imply a normalizer identification.','The cancelled rank criterion does not by itself determine the Lie normalizer. In characteristic2 the SL₂ Borel already shows that expected Lie self-normalization can fail.','corrected by himself later','C','The published relative rank theory in SGA3 XV/XVI has more hypotheses; the cancelled conclusion is not taken as a theorem.',['AG45-SGA3XVI','AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(110,[],45,[26],K2,'226–259','B is self-normalizing; B→N_G(LieB) topologically surjective.','A Borel subgroup of a reductive group is scheme-theoretically self-normalizing. The normalizer of its Lie algebra can be a nonreduced thickening; equality on geometric points is weaker than scheme equality or fppf surjectivity.','proof sketched','A','Conrad Corollary5.2.8 proves Borel self-normalization; the SL₂ characteristic2 tangent calculation distinguishes the Lie normalizer.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(111,[],45,[27],K2,'260–286','Pic⁰ near an abelian fibre; Picτ/Pic⁰ and torsion in NS.','For a smooth proper family with abelian fibres, the dual identity component is smooth; extension of this conclusion from one fibre or constancy of NS torsion needs openness, properness and flatness hypotheses. The preliminary finite-group bound is cancelled.','proof sketched','C','FGA VI and Kleiman §§5–6 give the modern Picard-component statements. They do not imply constant Néron–Severi rank in arbitrary smooth families.',['AG45-FGAVI','AG45-Kleiman2005'],confidence='medium')
cl(112,[],45,[29],K2,'287–321','Hyperalgebra of G_a: divided-power basis and coproduct.','For G_a over A, distributions γ_n dual to t^n satisfy γ_pγ_q=binomial(p+q,p)γ_(p+q) and Δγ_n=Σγ_i⊗γ_(n−i).','proved there','A','SGA3 VIIB formal-group/hyperalgebra duality contains the standard construction.',['AG45-SGA3VIIB'],'Coefficient extraction from(t⊗1+1⊗t)^(p+q) gives the binomial product; multiplication of monomials gives the dual coproduct.')
cl(113,[],45,[29],K2,'287–321','V(E), exponentials and group-like elements.','For a finite locally free module with the chosen V(E) variance, its additive formal group is dual to the divided-power coalgebra; group-like elements encode formal points.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 VIIB2.6 states the formal-group/bialgebra equivalence. The dual convention for E must be retained.',['AG45-SGA3VIIB'],'A point is an algebra map from the completed symmetric algebra; dualizing its multiplication identity yields Δx=x⊗x and ε(x)=1.')
cl(114,[],45,[30],K2,'322–405','Hyperalgebra of G_m, products X_pX_q.','For s=t−1, X_n dual to s^n has ΔX_n=ΣX_i⊗X_(n−i) and X_pX_q=Σ n!/((n−p)!(n−q)!(p+q−n)!)X_n for max(p,q)≤n≤p+q.','proved there','A','SGA3 VIIB duality applied to Δs=s⊗1+1⊗s+s⊗s gives this integral binomial distribution algebra.',['AG45-SGA3VIIB'],'math_checks.py:Dist_Gm_product tests49 polynomial identities and includes the universal subset-counting proof; X₁²=X₁+2X₂.')
cl(115,[],45,[32],K2,'423–484','X_n acting on t^m and(t−1)^m.','The invariant operator X_n satisfies X_n(t^m)=binomial(m,n)t^m and X_n((t−1)^m)=binomial(m,n)t^n(t−1)^(m−n); at t=1 the latter pairing isδ_nm.','proved there','A','These are the distribution actions of the multiplicative formal group; SGA3 VIIB supplies the formalism.',['AG45-SGA3VIIB'],'Extract z^n from f(t(1+z)): for f=t^m this is binomial(m,n)t^m; for f=(t−1)^m it is binomial(m,n)t^n(t−1)^(m−n). Evaluation at1 givesδ_nm.')
cl(116,[],45,[63],C1,'45–72','Completion of a nonnegatively graded algebra and continuous maps.','For A=⊕_(n≥0)A_n, the degree completion ∏A_n has Cauchy multiplication, finite in each fixed degree. Filtration-preserving homomorphisms into a complete separated target extend uniquely by continuity.','proved there','E','Classical filtered-algebra completion, treated in Bourbaki AlgebraIII; no Noetherian flatness claim is inferred from this elementary construction.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'For degreeN only N+1 pairs(i,j) contribute. Truncations form a Cauchy sequence, and separated completeness gives existence and uniqueness of the extension.')
cl(117,[],45,[72],C1,'114–127','Z[D]→Q(k) surjective with binary isometry relations.','For a characteristic≠2 field, GW(k) is generated by one-dimensional forms, and the relations can be generated by rank2 isometry relations (chain equivalence).','stated without proof','B','EKM §3 gives chain equivalence and the presentation of the Witt–Grothendieck ring; the 1961 letter asks that it be written out.',['AG45-EKM2008'],date='Letter22July1961; Delzant1962 is the adjoining printed development; EKM2008 is the opened exact modern reference.')
cl(118,[],45,[72],C1,'127–135','Binary isometry iff same degree1 and degree2 invariants.','Two nonsingular binary forms over a characteristic≠2 field are isometric iff their determinant square classes and quaternion/Hasse invariants agree, with the same convention on both sides.','stated without proof','B','EKM §§12–14 classifies binary forms through quadratic algebras and Clifford invariants. This does not identify every all-rank Clifford normalization.',['AG45-EKM2008'])
cl(119,[],45,[73],C1,'139–143','Kernel generated by e_(a+u+v)−e_(a+u)−e_(a+v)+e_a with uv=0.','The binary-relation presentation can be rewritten using vanishing quaternion symbols uv=0, after translating additive square-class notation and the rank convention.','stated without proof','B','EKM’s chain-equivalence/Steinberg presentation is the modern counterpart. The exact translation was checked algebraically and is separate from an associated-graded assertion.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'The relation is e_a(e_u−1)(e_v−1). Its rank and degree1 parts vanish; the degree2 symbol is u∪v. Vanishing of that symbol gives the corresponding binary relation under the preceding binary classification.')
cl(120,[],45,[73],C1,'145','Associated graded Q is the symmetric algebra modulo quadratic symbol-zero relations.','For the fundamental-ideal filtration over a characteristic≠2 field, the associated graded is Milnor K-theory mod2; quadratic Steinberg relations suffice. This conclusion does not formally follow from an arbitrary inhomogeneous ring presentation.','stated without proof','B','Milnor-conjecture results establish the field statement; EKM ChapterVII and §§42,44 give exact modern formulations. The inference written as immediate is a genuine extra mathematical step.',['AG45-EKM2008'])
cl(121,[],45,[73],C1,'145','Is grad²Q→H²(k,Z/2) always injective?','The degree2 norm-residue map K²_M(k)/2→Br(k)[2] is an isomorphism for characteristic≠2 fields; hence the corresponding I²/I³ map is injective.','open on the pages','B','Merkurjev’s degree2 theorem, presented and proved in EKM ChapterVIII, settles this precise field question.',['AG45-EKM2008'],date='Degree2 theorem1981; opened proof EKM2008. Earlier circulation of the manuscript question is fixed only by letter22July1961.')
cl(122,[],45,[73],C1,'147–163','Universal Stiefel–Whitney homomorphism from Q and degree1 data.','After specifying the target completed multiplicative formal group and its augmented-ring operations, total Stiefel–Whitney classes are determined on one-dimensional summands and respect binary relations. The stated universal target’s multiplication is not fully defined here.','proof sketched','C','EKM §5 gives the field total Stiefel–Whitney map; CCMT2013 §4 gives universal scheme classes. Neither identifies an undefined target ring merely from its underlying setZ×(1+H⁺).',['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-CCMT2013'],confidence='medium')
cl(123,[],45,[73],C1,'163','Serre suggests a finer pro2 cohomology.','Replacing the separable closure by a maximal pro2 extension changes the quotient Galois group; inflation compares its mod2 cohomology to the absolute Galois group. The asserted finer H² needs the exact extension definition and injectivity/surjectivity claim.','open on the pages','C','Milnor/norm-residue theory explains that degree1 symbols generate mod2 Galois cohomology, but the letter’s union-of-2-power-subextensions wording is not automatically a canonical Galois extension or a strict refinement.',['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-Harari2024'],confidence='medium')
cl(124,[],45,[74],C1,'167–171','λ_t(φ_a)=1+φ_a t; λ structure from ring and augmentation filtration.','Exterior powers give the usual λ-structure on GW(k); it is determined on line generators. Equality of a λ/γ filtration with the stated augmentation-ideal powers needs its exact convention, not merely the line formula.','stated without proof','B','The established Grothendieck–Witt λ-operations agree on line elements; EKM provides the underlying ring and modern λ-filtration comparisons require explicit γ versus exterior notation.',['AG45-EKM2008'],confidence='medium')
cl(125,[],45,[74],C1,'173–174','Everything should extend to a local ring with residue characteristic≠2; H* of π₁.','Unimodular quadratic forms over a local ring with2 invertible diagonalise, but full étale cohomology need not equal cohomology of π₁ for an arbitrary local scheme. The proposed wholesale extension requires a K(π,1) hypothesis or a change of cohomology.','open on the pages','C','Scheme Hasse–Witt theory uses étale cohomology, as in CCMT2013. The π₁-only formulation is too narrow without additional conditions.',['AG45-CCMT2013','AG45-EKV1993'],confidence='medium')
cl(126,[],45,[74],C1,'176–177','Classes for quadratic modules on an arbitrary scheme with2 invertible.','Unimodular symmetric bundles over schemes in which2 is invertible have étale Stiefel–Whitney/Hasse–Witt classes, with functoriality and splitting properties.','open on the pages','B','Jardine1989/1994 and EKV1993 develop the construction; CCMT2013 §§4–5 gives the classifying-topos comparison and exact low-degree formulas.',['AG45-CCMT2013','AG45-EKV1993'],date='Question dated22July1961; cited developments1989,1993,1994; CCMT arXiv21Jan2013.')
cl(127,[],45,[74],C1,'178–181','Compatibility with topological Stiefel–Whitney classes over C, by rank1 test.','For a complex algebraic symmetric bundle, comparison between étale and classical mod2 cohomology identifies the characteristic classes; descent to a local ring needs the direct-limit neighbourhood map and the chosen cohomology theory.','proof sketched','B','The universal class construction and comparison in EKV/Jardine provide the precise result. The sentence invoking GAGA does not itself establish a map from topological cohomology to arbitrary π₁ cohomology.',['AG45-EKV1993','AG45-CCMT2013'],confidence='medium')
cl(128,[],45,[75],C1,'185–187','Q′=Q+Z for degenerate forms, with multiplication formula.','In characteristic≠2, a finite-dimensional quadratic form splits into a nonsingular part and a zero radical. Its rank-retaining group completion is GW(k)⊕Z with product(f,m)(g,n)=(fg,rk(f)n+rk(g)m+mn).','stated without proof','E','Classical radical splitting verifies the stated formula. It does not create a functorial quadratic×quadratic product in characteristic2.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'Tensor the two decompositions: nonsingular partf⊗g; zero summands have dimensionsrk(f)n,rk(g)m,mn. These are exactly the displayed terms.')
cl(129,[],45,[75],C1,'189','H¹(S,μ₂)=A*/A*² for local A.','For local A, Pic(A)=0, so the flat Kummer sequence gives H¹(SpecA,μ₂)=A*/A*², in every characteristic.','stated without proof','A','Stacks59.28.3 proves flat Kummer exactness; the étale version needs2 invertible.',['AG45-StacksKummer'])
cl(130,[],45,[75],C1,'189','H²(S,μ₂)=₂H²(A,G_m).','For local A the same Kummer sequence gives H²_fppf(S,μ₂)≅H²_fppf(S,G_m)[2]. Calling this the Brauer group must distinguish cohomological from Azumaya conventions.','stated without proof','A','Stacks59.28.3 gives the exact cohomological assertion. No unproved equality Br=Br′ is inserted.',['AG45-StacksKummer'])
cl(131,[],45,[75],C1,'189','In characteristic2 H¹(S,Z/2)=A/wpA.','For affine S=SpecA in characteristic2, the Artin–Schreier sequence gives H¹_et(S,Z/2)=A/(x²−x)A and H^i_et(S,Z/2)=0 for i≥2.','stated without proof','A','Stacks59.63.1 gives the affine Artin–Schreier calculation; this verifies the author’s mixed-coefficient distinction.',['AG45-StacksAS'],'Surjectivity of x↦x²−x is étale local, derivative−1 invertible; positive cohomology of O on affine schemes vanishes, so the long exact sequence gives the result.')
cl(132,[],45,[75],C1,'189','Parity-dependent first invariant of orthogonal torsors.','Even regular rank yields a Z/2-valued component invariant, odd rank yields a μ₂-valued determinant/scaling invariant under the integral orthogonal conventions.','stated without proof','A','Conrad AppendixC, particularly RemarkC.3.7, confirms the parity distinction; this is the same construction as CL-SA18-45039/45040 with an additional explanatory occurrence.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
H=C/'H48-01_transcription.tex'
cl(133,[],48,[4],H,'63–95','Hom of pro-objects by lim colim; finite presentation of étale neighbourhoods.','For cofiltered pro-objects, Hom_Pro(X_i,Y_j)=lim_j colim_i Hom(X_i,Y_j). Finitely presented étale objects commute with filtered affine limits, providing the strict-localisation calculation.','proof sketched','A','SGA4 VIII §§7.2–7.9 gives the exact geometric use. The X_j versus X′_j typo does not change the required variance.',['AG45-SGA4VIII'])
cl(134,[],48,[4],H,'106–118','Fibre functor is a filtered colimit of representables.','A left-exact fibre functor on the relevant small étale category is represented by a pro-object built from its filtered category of elements; the stated finite-limit hypotheses ensure filteredness.','proof sketched','A','SGA4 IV/VIII gives this standard construction of geometric points and their stalk functors.',['AG45-SGA4VIII'],'Given two pointed neighbourhoods take their fibre product; parallel arrows are equalised after a smaller pointed neighbourhood. These are exactly the filtered-category conditions.')
cl(135,[],48,[6],H,'176–208','SGA VIII7.9: antiequivalence between points and fibre functors.','Geometric points and their specialization morphisms, with the convention on separably closed residue fields used in SGA4, form the category opposite to étale-topos points/fibre functors.','proof sketched','A','SGA4 VIII7.9 is the direct published counterpart of this reused typescript. The rest of H48 is an editorial chapter outline and is not converted into asserted theorems.',['AG45-SGA4VIII'],date='SGA4 seminar1963–1964; publishedLNM270 in1972.')

